BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sphera-media-lab-2026-open-call-discourse@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sphera Media Lab 2026: Open Call for Masterclasses and Ke
 ynotes
DESCRIPTION:European Lab and the Sphera Network are opening submissions for
  the discourse programme of the next Sphera Media Lab\, the annual forum f
 or people defending independent media in Europe. THEMES: technological ind
 ependence (sovereignty\, tools\, mutualisation)\; economic independence (c
 ooperative models)\; editorial independence (press freedom\, resisting the
  far right). FORMATS SOUGHT: masterclasses\, led by an expert sharing adva
 nced knowledge\, technique and hands-on insight\; and keynotes\, inspiring
  talks grounded in a specific case study. WHO CAN PROPOSE: media outlets\,
  associations\, researchers and professionals. The organisers state they p
 ay particular attention to parity and diversity across their programmes an
 d ask that proposals take those criteria into account. WHAT IS COVERED: se
 lected contributors travel to Brussels with transport\, accommodation and 
 a fee covered. THE EVENT: Sphera Media Lab 2026 runs on 3 December 2026 at
  LaVallee in Brussels\, a full day of conferences\, masterclasses and work
 shops for independent media professionals from across Europe\, alongside t
 he premiere of a documentary on threats facing European journalists. APPLI
 CATION: submit your masterclass or keynote proposal via the Typeform at ar
 ty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM before 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation: 
 European Lab (Arty Farty) and the Sphera Network\, coordinated by Medianes
 \nLocation: LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. 
 Proposals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should
  reflect a European perspective.\nAward: Selected contributors receive a f
 ee plus travel to Brussels and accommodation\, all covered by the organise
 rs. The fee amount is not published in the call. No submission fee.\nDurat
 ion: One-day event on 3 December 2026\; contributors deliver a single mast
 erclass or keynote\n\nMore: https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://arty-farty.typeform.com/to/xnDikGGM?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:LaVallee\, Rue Adolphe Lavallee 39\, 1080 Brussels\, Belgium. Prop
 osals are accepted regardless of where you are based\, but they should ref
 lect a European perspective.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:propublica-emerging-reporters-2026-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: ProPublica Emerging Reporters Program 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:ProPublica's Emerging Reporters Program is a remote mentorship 
 and professional-development programme for aspiring investigative journali
 sts\, built specifically for students for whom investigative journalism is
  financially out of reach. Five students are selected each year. WHAT FELL
 OWS RECEIVE: a $9\,000 stipend paid in two $4\,500 instalments (start and 
 midpoint)\, one-to-one mentoring from ProPublica journalists\, training an
 d resources\, and an all-expenses-paid trip to a journalism conference suc
 h as the Investigative Reporters and Editors conference (accommodation and
  expenses covered by ProPublica). ELIGIBILITY: undergraduate juniors or se
 niors enrolled during the 2026-27 academic year\, who can demonstrate fina
 ncial need and early dedication to journalism through internships\, campus
  publications or local news work. Students from groups traditionally under
 represented in journalism are especially encouraged to apply. HARD RESTRIC
 TION: applicants must be US citizens or US residents and able to file US t
 axes\; the required W-9 needs a valid Social Security number or ITIN\, and
  ProPublica states it makes no exceptions. APPLICATION: resume or CV\, cur
 rent academic transcript\, expected graduation date\, essays on investigat
 ive-journalism interest\, career goals\, financial circumstances and barri
 ers faced\, plus three journalism projects with URLs or PDFs and a backsto
 ry of up to 200 words each. Applications close 17 August 2026 at 9:00am ET
 .\n\nOrganisation: ProPublica\nLocation: Remote\, with an all-expenses-pai
 d trip to a journalism conference. RESTRICTED: US citizens or US residents
  only\, and participants must supply a completed W-9 with a valid SSN or I
 TIN (no exceptions).\nAward: $9\,000 stipend\, paid as two instalments of 
 $4\,500 (one at the start of the programme\, one at the midpoint)\, plus a
  fully funded journalism conference trip with accommodation and expenses c
 overed. No application fee.\nDuration: Late September 2026 through June 20
 27\, remote\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/propublica/jobs/4577
 283006?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/propublica/jobs/4577283006?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an all-expenses-paid trip to a journalism conference
 . RESTRICTED: US citizens or US residents only\, and participants must sup
 ply a completed W-9 with a valid SSN or ITIN (no exceptions).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:surels-place-residency-2027-winter@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260901
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260902
SUMMARY:Deadline: Surel's Place: Month-Long Artist Residency (September 202
 6 deadline)
DESCRIPTION:Surel's Place is a residency in Garden City\, Idaho with a two-
 fold mission: to give artists the time\, shelter and professional support 
 their work deserves\, and to give the public accessible art experiences in
 cluding performances\, readings\, exhibits and workshops. In exchange for 
 the residency\, artists offer art experiences back to the community. OPEN 
 TO professional visual\, literary and performance artists: painters\, writ
 ers\, musicians\, architects\, filmmakers\, choreographers\, any artist wh
 o needs a place to focus. LIMITS: because of the property's constraints an
 d proximity to neighbours\, it cannot accommodate loud or very messy proce
 sses such as welding or blacksmithing. The organisers also note that predo
 minantly conceptual work\, work that must be explained to attract a viewer
 \, may not be met with as much enthusiasm by the jurors\, and that they we
 lcome artists who want to connect with an audience. Each cycle\, one of th
 e five residencies includes outreach to local schools through the PARTNER 
 Schools Program. REQUIREMENTS: maintain residence at the home and studio (
 no studio-use-only residencies)\; use the residency in a focused way towar
 ds a stated goal with no other employment or work happening simultaneously
 \; visual artists give a show of and/or lecture about their residency work
 \, writers give a reading and discussion session\; all artists offer at le
 ast one public workshop (2 to 3 hours\, minimal lecturing)\; visual artist
 s donate one piece of art to Surel's Place for future fundraising\; writer
 s allow Surel's Place to publish a free-standing excerpt of their residenc
 y work in books\, broadsides\, posters or similar. SALES COMMISSION applie
 s as set out in the funding notes. HOUSE RULES: the non-smoking requiremen
 t is non-negotiable and any evidence of smoking is grounds for early termi
 nation. Partners are welcome but children and pets cannot be accommodated\
 , no more than two adults may live there during a residency\, and partners
  wanting concurrent residencies must apply and be accepted separately. ELI
 GIBILITY: applicants must be 25 or older and non-smokers. APPLY by email t
 o info@surelsplace.org with the online Artist in Residence application plu
 s\, for VISUAL ARTISTS\, a cover letter introducing yourself and saying wh
 y you want to be at Surel's Place specifically\, a proposal including a wo
 rkshop outline\, a CV\, electronic images of 4 to 6 pieces of work and an 
 image list (title\, size\, medium\, year)\; for LITERARY applicants\, cove
 r letter\, proposal with workshop outline\, CV and PDF work samples with y
 our name and page number in the top right corner (fiction\, prose or scrip
 t no more than 10 typed double-spaced pages\; poetry no more than six poem
 s and no more than 10 pages)\; for OTHER MEDIUMS\, cover letter\, proposal
  with workshop outline\, CV and electronic samples (contact Surel's Place 
 to discuss sample requirements for your medium). Applications are reviewed
  by juries of accomplished artists\, writers and fine arts professionals\,
  and all applicants are notified by email.\n\nOrganisation: Surel's Place\
 nLocation: In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents 
 must live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.\nAward: Fre
 e rent\, utilities and wi-fi\, a living stipend of USD 100 per week\, a US
 D 300 travel stipend\, use of well-maintained bicycles\, free or discounte
 d tickets to art events and movies\, marketing through Surel's Place chann
 els\, and event hosting including logistics and installation help. Each re
 sident also gets a local host to orient them and host their public events.
  No application fee. NOTE: Surel's Place takes a commission on sales arisi
 ng from the residency\, 35% of retail price on artwork\, 45% on ticketed e
 vents that are offsite or need contracted services (calculated after admin
 istrative expenses)\, and 15% on books\, CDs or other merchandise tangenti
 al to the artist's medium. A surcharge may apply to cover additional utili
 ty costs if a partner joins.\nDuration: One month (longer may be possible 
 if justified in the application). The 1 September deadline covers residenc
 ies in January to May of the following year\, beginning four months after 
 the deadline\; the 1 March deadline covers July to November of the same ye
 ar.\n\nMore: https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://surelsplace.org/month-long-residencies/?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person in Garden City\, Idaho\, USA (Boise area). Residents mus
 t live at the home and studio\; no studio-only residencies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:princeton-arts-fellowship-2027-2029@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260909
SUMMARY:Deadline: Princeton Arts Fellowship 2027-2029 (Lewis Center for the
  Arts)
DESCRIPTION:The Princeton Arts Fellowship places early-career artists at th
 e Lewis Center for the Arts for two academic years on a full-time salaried
  appointment\, on the conviction that exposure to the arts helps us make s
 ense of our lives and the lives of our neighbours. ELIGIBLE APPLICANTS are
  early-career composers\, conductors\, musicians\, choreographers\, playwr
 ights\, designers\, directors and performance artists. IMPORTANT FOR THIS 
 CYCLE: applications are accepted only for the Program in Dance\, the Progr
 am in Theater and Music Theater\, and the Department of Music. Fellows typ
 ically teach one undergraduate course per semester (subject to approval by
  the Office of the Dean of the Faculty)\, or in some cases take on an arti
 stic assignment with students in lieu of a class\; prior teaching experien
 ce is NOT required. Beyond that\, fellows are expected to engage with the 
 Princeton community through advising students\, talks\, workshops\, exhibi
 tions or other events\, and have time to pursue their own artistic practic
 e and scholarly research. NOT ELIGIBLE: the appointment cannot be used to 
 fund work leading to a PhD or any advanced degree\; holders of Princeton P
 hDs cannot apply\; past recipients of the Hodder Fellowship cannot apply\;
  and anyone with a sustained and continuous relationship with Princeton is
  excluded (an occasional and sporadic relationship is fine). Fellows may n
 ot hold concurrent teaching positions at other institutions during the app
 ointment. You may apply once per application cycle. APPLICATION via Interf
 olio: a CV\; a 750-word proposal broken into how you would use the fellows
 hip to develop your work (250 words)\, what you might teach or pursue with
  undergraduates (250 words)\, and your approach to building community with
  people of different backgrounds\, experiences\, perspectives and artistic
  practices through your practice\, teaching and/or research (250 words)\; 
 contact information for one reference\; and work samples with a 150-word s
 tatement on how they relate to your proposal\, submitted as links in a PDF
 . WORK SAMPLE SPECS: Dance (choreography)\, one to three links to performa
 nces\, rehearsals or research\, continuous footage only\, no trailers\, ph
 otographs or promotional material\, with cue points of no more than 3 to 4
  minutes per link and no more than 10 minutes total. Music\, one to two sc
 ores and/or links to performance examples totalling 10 minutes\, video opt
 ional. Theater\, up to 15 pages of writing sample and/or up to 10 design i
 mages (photos or renderings) and/or a video or audio reel with cue points\
 , no more than 10 minutes to watch. The deadline is 8 September 2026 at 11
 :59 pm EDT\; receipt of applications cannot be confirmed and late applicat
 ions are not accepted. FAQ and examples of building community in artistic 
 practice\, teaching and research: arts.princeton.edu/fellowships/faq.\n\nO
 rganisation: Princeton University\, Lewis Center for the Arts\nLocation: I
 n person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees are expecte
 d to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, engaged i
 nteraction with students. These positions are not eligible for sponsorship
  of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.\nAward: USD 93\,000 salary
  per year for each of two academic years\, as a full-time 24-month appoint
 ment at the rank of Associate Professional Specialist. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Two academic years\, 2027-2028 and 2028-2029. Applications ope
 n 1 July 2026 and close 8 September 2026\; finalists attend an on-campus i
 nterview during the week of 7 December 2026.\n\nMore: https://apply.interf
 olio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://apply.interfolio.com/187043?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantde
 sk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In person on campus at Princeton\, New Jersey\, USA. Appointees ar
 e expected to be full and active members of the community with frequent\, 
 engaged interaction with students. These positions are not eligible for sp
 onsorship of an H-1B visa requiring consular processing.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collar-works-elizabeth-murray-residency-2027@artificialnouveau.github.i
 o
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collar Works: Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency (EMAR) 20
 27
DESCRIPTION:The Elizabeth Murray Artist Residency gives emerging\, underrep
 resented and established artists an immersive\, communal atmosphere for ar
 t-making and dialogue on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, New York\, the clas
 sic farmhouse where the late Elizabeth Murray spent more than two decades 
 of summers and kept her studio in the large\, cathedral-like dairy barn. M
 urray\, who died in 2007\, won a Skowhegan Medal in Painting in 1986\, a M
 acArthur grant in 1999 and a MoMA career retrospective in 2005\; her husba
 nd Bob Holman is a poet and arts activist who founded the Bowery Poetry Cl
 ub. In 2017 the Murray-Holman family partnered with Collar Works\, a nonpr
 ofit contemporary arts organisation in Troy\, New York\, to build a summer
  residency carrying the legacy forward. EMAR is explicitly designed around
  the critical needs of emerging and underrepresented artists and of artist
 s with children\, with an active commitment to diversity\, equity\, access
 ibility and inclusion\, and describes itself as a safe space for creative 
 risk-taking\, personal growth and respite. Work is primarily in visual and
  literary arts\, though the programme supports new contemporary work acros
 s many art forms. TWO TRACKS: the INDIVIDUAL RESIDENCY takes up to five ar
 tists per session (including onsite artist-staff) for two weeks\, with 24-
 hour access to semi-private studios in the barn\, a private bedroom in the
  farmhouse\, shared bathrooms\, communal living room\, kitchen and a libra
 ry holding Elizabeth Murray's personal art book collection. The FAMILY RES
 IDENCY invites three to four artists and their families for one week\, wit
 h a private bedroom (families may need to room together)\, a semi-private 
 studio in the same barn\, and full-day childcare running as a parallel cre
 ative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, focused on art and nature prog
 ramming: while parent artists get uninterrupted studio time\, children wor
 k in their own EMAR Kids Art Studio\, explore outside through guided activ
 ities\, play\, swim\, read and take guided field trips to local farms and 
 museums. Partners are welcome to join and help with childcare\, but the pr
 ogramme is committed to fully supporting artists who want or need to atten
 d solo with their children\, and Collar Works works case by case with each
  artist to put the necessary support and equipment in place before arrival
  given the rural setting. APPLICATION requires artist information\, a stat
 ement of intent (250 to 500 words)\, an artist biography (250 words)\, ten
  examples of recent work (visual arts as JPEGs up to 2MB each\, with a PDF
  of links or a curated URL page for video and media works including any pa
 sswords\; literary arts as up to three poems totalling max six pages\, or 
 max ten pages of prose) and a list of works with title\, medium\, date and
  size. Applications go through Submittable and close 1 October 2026 (the S
 ubmittable listing shows a 2 October 2026 06:00 close in its own timezone)
 . Questions and technical assistance: info@collarworks.org.\n\nOrganisatio
 n: Collar Works\nLocation: In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Wash
 ington County\, New York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray
  and Bob Holman). Collar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.\nAward:
  Fully supported: there is no cost to participate for accepted artists. Pr
 ivate bedroom accommodation with shared bathrooms in the farmhouse\, 24-ho
 ur access to a semi-private studio in the barn that housed Elizabeth Murra
 y's studio\, all food provided (dinner cooked and eaten communally)\, and 
 use of the property including a swimming pool\, pond and 77 acres of field
 s and woods. Accepted artists also receive a USD 500 stipend to use at the
 ir discretion towards travel\, materials or other associated costs. The Fa
 mily Residency additionally provides free full-day (9am to 3pm) childcare 
 as a parallel creative residency for children aged 5 to 12\, plus a dedica
 ted Kids Art Studio. APPLICATION FEE: USD 35 (the first month of the appli
 cation period was free\; the fee applies from 1 August 2026).\nDuration: T
 wo weeks for individual artists\, one week for families. 2027 individual s
 essions: Session 1\, 15 to 29 May 2027\; Session 2\, 1 to 15 June 2027. 20
 27 family session: Session 3\, 19 to 26 June 2027. Finalist interviews Nov
 ember 2026\; final notification December 2026.\n\nMore: https://collarwork
 s.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-artist-residency-2027?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://collarworks.submittable.com/submit/360246/elizabeth-murray-arti
 st-residency-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referra
 l
LOCATION:In person on a 77-acre farm in Granville\, Washington County\, New
  York\, USA (the former summer home of Elizabeth Murray and Bob Holman). C
 ollar Works itself is based in Troy\, New York.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:sundance-episodic-program-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260825
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260826
SUMMARY:Deadline: Sundance Institute: Episodic Program and Lab 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Sundance Institute Episodic Program is an entry point for e
 merging to mid-career writers and creators working across all scripted ser
 ies platforms\, combining a lab experience with customised support to move
  projects and careers forward. The Episodic Lab lets writers workshop an o
 riginal pilot and develop their writing and pitching skills: working with 
 showrunners\, producers and executives\, fellows take part in one-on-one s
 tory meetings\, pitching sessions\, craft workshops and writers rooms. Up 
 to ten applicants are selected\, either for the in-person Lab (preceded by
  virtual workshops) or for a fully virtual Intensive with a creative advis
 or. ELIGIBILITY: applicants must be 18 or older\; the script must be the a
 pplicant's original creation or adapted from optioned source material and 
 written in English\; the draft must be revised or new\, not already produc
 ed\, not sold to a third party and not distributed online\; and applicants
  cannot have previously had an episodic project (pilot or pitch) produced 
 by a studio or network. Open to international and U.S. participants\, subj
 ect to travel guidelines. Up to two writers per project may participate\, 
 with one named as primary applicant. APPLICATION: submitted via FilmFreewa
 y\, including writer bios (250 words each)\, a series logline (50 words)\,
  pilot synopsis (500 words)\, one-page series overview (500 words)\, perso
 nal statement (250 words)\, community connection\, thematic statement (250
  words)\, characters and core relationships (250 words)\, episodic vision\
 , optional video samples (up to three links\, 10 minutes total)\, and the 
 full pilot script as a PDF in standard screenplay format. Demographic ques
 tions are optional and do not affect consideration. Candidates may submit 
 up to three applications\, with a separate application and USD 50 fee for 
 each project. Submissions opened 13 July 2026 and close 25 August 2026 at 
 6:00 p.m. PT. Finalists are notified by email through FilmFreeway around m
 id to late March 2027 (FilmFreeway lists 31 March 2027)\, and the list of 
 selected writers and projects is published at sundance.org/epi after 15 Ap
 ril 2027. Finalists must return a Fellow Agreement\, a Participation Agree
 ment and any other required documents within five days. Questions: episodi
 cprogram@sundance.org.\n\nOrganisation: Sundance Institute\nLocation: Open
  to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes place in the At
 lanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive track is fully o
 nline.\nAward: No stipend or honorarium for participation. For the in-pers
 on Lab\, Sundance covers round-trip basic economy airfare from the airport
  nearest the fellow's residence\, shared housing with a private room and b
 ath\, and ground transport to and from the airport (fellows within 100 mil
 es of the Lab get ground transport only). Meals\, in-room charges\, travel
  insurance\, luggage fees and taxes are the fellow's own cost. One selecte
 d project also receives a separate monetary grant from the Alfred P. Sloan
  Foundation for a series with an illuminating emphasis on science\, includ
 ing medicine\, energy\, technology or invention. APPLICATION FEE: USD 50 n
 onrefundable processing fee per project submitted.\nDuration: In-person La
 b in Spring 2027 (event dates 15 to 20 April 2027) preceded by virtual wor
 kshops\; the Intensive track convenes virtually in Summer 2027.\n\nMore: h
 ttps://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://filmfreeway.com/SundanceEpisodicLab2027?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open to international and U.S. applicants. In-person Lab takes pla
 ce in the Atlanta\, Georgia area\, USA in Spring 2027\; the Intensive trac
 k is fully online.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:beinecke-short-term-fellowships-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260815
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260816
SUMMARY:Deadline: Beinecke Library Short-Term Research Fellowships 2027-202
 8 (Yale)
DESCRIPTION:The Beinecke Library Fellowship Program offers competitive shor
 t-term fellowships to support research projects that substantively engage 
 with Yale Library Special Collections materials. WHO CAN APPLY: academic a
 nd independent scholars\, locally and globally\, with or without an instit
 utional affiliation\; the library explicitly welcomes applicants regardles
 s of institutional association\, race\, cultural background\, ability\, se
 xual orientation\, gender or socioeconomic status. Applications using trad
 itional archival and bibliographic research methods are encouraged\, and s
 o are creative\, interdisciplinary and non-traditional approaches to worki
 ng with the collections\, which makes this a live option for artists and w
 riters whose practice is research-driven. WHAT YOU GET: USD 5\,000 per mon
 th for an onsite research visit of between two weeks and two months\, paid
  at the start of the fellowship whenever possible (international fellows s
 hould expect payment to take several weeks)\; fellows pay their own taxes 
 on the award. TIMELINE: applications for the 2027-2028 awards opened 1 Jun
 e 2026 and close 15 August 2026\, via Interfolio\; all decisions are notif
 ied by 31 January 2027\; research must be conducted between 1 July 2027 an
 d 30 June 2028\, and awards cannot be deferred.\n\nOrganisation: Beinecke 
 Rare Book & Manuscript Library\, Yale University\nLocation: Onsite at Yale
  Library Special Collections\, New Haven\, Connecticut\, USA. Open to acad
 emic and independent scholars locally and globally\, regardless of institu
 tional association.\nAward: USD 5\,000 per month for research visits betwe
 en two weeks and two months. Funding is paid at the beginning of the fello
 wship where possible (payment to international fellows often takes several
  weeks)\; fellows are responsible for taxes on the award. Free to apply.\n
 Duration: Two weeks to two months of onsite research\, conducted between 1
  July 2027 and 30 June 2028\; awards cannot be deferred\n\nMore: https://b
