DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: NEU Commission 2026
DESCRIPTION:Page Not Found announces the third edition of the NEU Commissio
 n\, which intends to support the emergence of alternative voices within co
 ntemporary art. It targets a group currently under-represented in Europe: 
 art students with limited financial means who are required to provide a gu
 arantee for their visa and to pay high non-EU tuition fees\, economical ba
 rriers to their participation in art and knowledge production in The Nethe
 rlands. Page Not Found will commission three art students from non-Europea
 n countries to each write an essay or a free-form text in English. Each se
 lected writer receives EUR 2\,000 for the commission\, provided as an adva
 nce payment in September 2026\; the commissioned texts are published at a 
 later date. OPEN CALL: only students enrolled in Dutch art academies who a
 re NOT nationals of the European Economic Area are eligible. The commissio
 ned text does not need to be submitted when answering the open call\; the 
 jury selects recipients based on their previous writing and financial need
 . Review the Rules of the Open Call and the FAQ before applying. Deadline:
  Friday 31 July 2026\, 23:59 CEST (Amsterdam).\n\nOrganisation: Page Not F
 ound\nLocation: The Netherlands\; open to non-EEA-national students enroll
 ed at Dutch art academies.\nAward: EUR 2\,000 each to three commissioned w
 riters\, paid as an advance in September 2026.\nDuration: One-time commiss
 ion\; commissioned texts published at a later date\n\nMore: http://neu-com
 mission.page-not-found.nl/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
URL:http://neu-commission.page-not-found.nl/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:The Netherlands\; open to non-EEA-national students enrolled at Du
 tch art academies.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pulitzer-center-ai-accountability-fellowships-2026-2027@artificialnouve
 au.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pulitzer Center: AI Accountability Fellowships 2026-2027
DESCRIPTION:The AI Accountability Fellowships support journalists working o
 n in-depth AI accountability stories that examine how governments and corp
 orations use predictive\, generative and surveillance technologies to guid
 e decisions in policing\, medicine\, social welfare\, criminal justice\, h
 iring and more. Designed for reporters from all beats\, desks and formats\
 , the fellowship asks applicants to propose a concrete reporting project (
 with evidence of pre-reporting) that uses approaches such as data analysis
 \, records requests and shoe-leather reporting to examine the real-world i
 mpact of algorithms. For the first time this year\, the fellowship also in
 cludes funding\, mentorship and training to develop and execute an impact/
 engagement plan to reach strategic audiences. SUPPORT: up to USD 25\,000 p
 er fellow (up to USD 20\,000 reporting plus USD 5\,000 engagement)\, paid 
 in three installments\; freelancers may budget up to one third as a stipen
 d\, while newsrooms cover staff salaries. Fellows also receive mentors\, s
 pecialized training\, pro bono legal and public records support\, and a la
 sting community of peers. ELIGIBILITY: staff or freelance journalists acro
 ss print\, radio\, video and multimedia\, able to work collaboratively\; r
 eporters may be based anywhere (fellowship is remote). Experience reportin
 g on AI is not required\, but a track record of in-depth\, impactful repor
 ting and investigative/data/explanatory experience is valued. Small teams 
 may apply with a designated lead Fellow. REQUIREMENTS: a mandatory monthly
  1.5-2 hour meeting\, at least one community call\, engagement with other 
 fellows\, and sharing of methodologies and lessons learned\; fellowship co
 mmunication\, meetings and training are in English\, but fellows may publi
 sh in any language. APPLY: submit a 500-word statement of purpose\, a 500-
 word project description with reporting plan\, an engagement plan\, a budg
 et\, three links to recent work\, a letter of commitment from a publishing
  media organization (or editor support letter for staff)\, three reference
 s and a CV. Funded with support from the MacArthur Foundation\, Luminate\,
  Omidyar Network and others. Contact: reacheditorial@pulitzercenter.org. D
 eadline: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EDT.\n\nOrganisation: Pulitzer Center\nLoc
 ation: Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is r
 emote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing a
  broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.\nAward: Up to USD 25\,000 per 
 fellow\, paid in three installments (up to USD 20\,000 for reporting and U
 SD 5\,000 for engagement activities)\; covers records requests\, travel\, 
 data analysis and engagement costs. Freelancers may budget up to one third
  of the total as a stipend\; newsrooms are expected to pay staff salaries.
  No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months\, expected September 2026 to Jul
 y 2027\n\nMore: https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunitie
 s-journalists/ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://pulitzercenter.org/grants-fellowships/opportunities-journalists
 /ai-accountability-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; reporters may be based anywhere and the fellowship is 
 remote. Proposals from the Global South and from journalists representing 
 a broad array of backgrounds are encouraged.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iwmf-kari-howard-fund-narrative-journalism-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260712
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260713
SUMMARY:Deadline: IWMF: The Kari Howard Fund for Narrative Journalism 2026
DESCRIPTION:Established in memory of editor Kari Howard\, who championed na
 rrative journalism that wove the music of everyday life into stories illum
 inating the most important issues of the day. Offered by Kari's family and
  friends together with the International Women's Media Foundation\, the fu
 nd supports narrative journalism projects. Grants average around USD 5\,00
 0 (based on previous years). ELIGIBILITY: open to women and nonbinary jour
 nalists. Applicants may be a print journalist or a print journalist leadin
 g a multi-media team\, and may be freelance or staff\; they may apply indi
 vidually or as part of a multi-format team. Professional journalism must b
 e the applicant's primary profession\, with three or more years of profess
 ional experience (internships do not count). Applicants must show proof of
  interest from an editor or a proven track record of publication in promin
 ent media outlets. Applicants must apply and publish in English\; projects
  published anywhere in the world are eligible\, and stories may be publish
 ed digitally (not necessarily in a newspaper or magazine). Any multi-forma
 t reporting must supplement a printed project. All reporting and publishin
 g must be completed within six months of the award. Applicants receive a d
 ecision by early September. DEADLINE: 12 July 2026\, 11:59pm EST.\n\nOrgan
 isation: International Women's Media Foundation (IWMF)\nLocation: Worldwid
 e\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in the world. Open
  to women and nonbinary journalists.\nAward: Grants average around USD 5\,
 000 (based on previous years). No application fee.\nDuration: All reportin
 g and publishing must be completed within six months of the award\n\nMore:
  https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c703381/
 the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://iwmf.submittable.com/submit/c65a15f2-9ff5-4c2b-b2a3-2d897c70338
 1/the-kari-howard-fund-for-narrative-journalism-2026?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide\; projects reported and published in English anywhere in
  the world. Open to women and nonbinary journalists.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:berggruen-prize-essay-competition-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260817
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260818
SUMMARY:Deadline: Berggruen Prize Essay Competition 2026: A New Axial Age?
DESCRIPTION:Annual essay competition from the Berggruen Institute\, a compa
 nion to the Berggruen Prize for Philosophy & Culture\, seeking new thinkin
 g and cross-cultural perspectives on fundamental philosophical questions o
 f significance for contemporary life and the future. It looks for essays i
 n the tradition of Rousseau\, Montaigne\, and Emerson (models cited includ
 e Frankfurt\, Nagel\, Sontag\, Fukuyama\, and recent Noema pieces)\, not p
 eer-reviewed academic work: novel ideas\, clearly argued in compelling way
 s for intellectually serious readers. THEME for 2026: 'A New Axial Age?' R
 eferencing Karl Jaspers's Axial Age (c. 800-200 BCE)\, the prompt asks whe
 ther civilisation is again at a 'hinge of history' undergoing a pivotal tr
 ansformation of consciousness driven by rapid technological change and its
  social\, economic\, and political consequences. Suggested (non-exhaustive
 ) angles: the foundational ideas and institutions underpinning present and
  future societies\; the philosophical implications of the current era\; th
 e future of human evolution and life amid the ecological crisis\; shared c
 onceptual foundations for a common future\; global transformation driven b
 y technology and the rise of AI\; the end of the global order and its rami
 fications\; and the future of life amid biotechnology and synthetic life. 
 Entrants may choose their own topic\; for 2026 creative fiction is accepte
 d. ELIGIBILITY: open to the public\; not for academic papers and not gover
 ned by academic disciplinary standards or affiliations. Length up to 10\,0
 00 words in English or 15\,000 characters in Chinese (excluding diagrams\,
  tables\, endnotes\, bibliography\, or authorship declaration). One submis
 sion per year per entrant\, in either English or Chinese\; co-authorship a
 llowed. Submissions must be original and previously unpublished. AI USAGE:
  generative AI must not be used to create content or substitute original t
 hought\, and entrants must engage primary sources rather than AI summaries
 \; AI is permitted only for proofreading/grammar\, as quoted supporting ma
 terial when AI itself is the subject of the research\, or for translating 
 an essay into English or Chinese\, and all AI use (including AI-assisted t
 ranslation) must be disclosed on the front page or the entry is disqualifi
 ed. JUDGING: originality\, clarity\, and style. Essays are due by 23:59 PT
  on 17 August 2026\; PDF only. Chinese-language submissions use a separate
  page. Inquiries to essay@berggruen.org.\n\nOrganisation: Berggruen Instit
 ute\nLocation: Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and C
 hinese submissions)\nAward: USD 50\,000 for the winning English essay and 
 USD 50\,000 for the winning Chinese essay. If there is more than one winne
 r in a language category\, the prize is split evenly. Winning essays are p
 ublished through BI Press or Noema. No entry fee.\nDuration: One-off compe
 tition\; submissions open 17 March 2026 and close 17 August 2026\n\nMore: 
 https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://berggruen.org/eu/essay-competition-open?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote/online\; open to the public worldwide (English and Chinese 
 submissions)
END:VEVENT
