BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wheeler-centre-next-chapter-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Wheeler Centre: The Next Chapter 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Next Chapter is The Wheeler Centre's flagship writing progr
 am\, designed to elevate Australian stories that are underrepresented and 
 to nurture a new wave of writers to tell them. Each fellow receives a AUD 
 $15\,000 stipend and is paired with a handpicked mentor for a twelve-month
  period to progress a major writing project. Fellows also receive a reside
 ncy at Varuna\, The National Writers' House\; an end-of-year publishing in
 tensive at The Wheeler Centre\; and meaningful opportunities to connect wi
 th peers\, agents\, publishers\, and readers. One fellowship place is dedi
 cated specifically to a poet: The Malcolm Robertson Fellowship. 2027 judge
 s: Melissa Lucashenko\, Micaela Sahhar\, Cher Tan\, and Melanie Saward. EL
 IGIBILITY: Australian writers. No specific genre restrictions stated\; the
  program focuses on stories that are not currently being told and voices t
 hat are underrepresented. TIMELINE: submissions open 2 June 2026\; submiss
 ions close 31 July 2026 (5pm)\; 2027 Next Chapter Fellows announced 10 Nov
 ember 2026. Questions: thenextchapter@wheelercentre.com. Apply at https://
 www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter.\n\nOrganisation: The Wheeler Centr
 e\nLocation: Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Na
 tional Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Whe
 eler Centre.\nAward: AUD $15\,000 stipend per fellow. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months.\n\nMore: https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chap
 ter?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.wheelercentre.com/the-next-chapter?utm_source=artificialnou
 veaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Melbourne\, Australia\; includes residency at Varuna\, The Nationa
 l Writers' House\, and an end-of-year publishing intensive at The Wheeler 
 Centre.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:richell-prize-emerging-writers-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260713
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260714
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers 2026
DESCRIPTION:The Richell Prize for Emerging Writers\, presented by the Emerg
 ing Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\, is an annual prize for unpu
 blished writers of adult fiction and adult narrative non-fiction. The winn
 er receives AUD $10\,000 and a 12-month mentorship with a Hachette Austral
 ia publisher\, who will work with the writer to develop their manuscript\,
  with first option to consider the finished work for publication. Long- an
 d short-listed writers are also eligible to apply for a Casa Morphosi Resi
 dency in Abruzzo\, Italy. The prize was established in memory of Hachette 
 Australia CEO Matt Richell (d. 2014). ELIGIBILITY: must currently reside i
 n Australia (Australian citizens\, permanent residents\, and long-term vis
 a holders residing in Australia\; those residing overseas are not eligible
 )\; unpublished writers only -- no previous publication by a publishing ho
 use (including small press or independent publishers in any genre)\; publi
 cation in anthologies\, literary journals\, scholarly works\, writing PhD 
 projects\, or self-publishing does not disqualify. ELIGIBLE GENRES: adult 
 fiction (including short story collections) and adult narrative non-fictio
 n (including memoir and essay collections). Poetry\, scripts\, plays\, pic
 ture books\, practical non-fiction\, and works for children or young adult
 s are not eligible. SUBMISSION FORMAT: single PDF comprising the first thr
 ee chapters (maximum 20\,000 words)\, a one-page synopsis\, and a chapter 
 breakdown of up to two A4 pages\; plus a statement (up to 750 words) on ho
 w winning the prize will further your writing career\, entered in the onli
 ne form. Manuscript must be complete or intended to be completed\; does no
 t need to be finished at time of submission. A4\, double-spaced\, 12pt Rom
 an font (e.g. Garamond or Times New Roman)\, 3cm margins\, page numbers. T
 IMELINE: prize opened 23 April 2026\; closes 13 July 2026 at 11:59pm AEST\
 ; longlist announced 11 September 2026\; shortlist 16 October 2026\; winne
 r announced 6 November 2026. Administered with support from Simpsons Solic
 itors. Apply at https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/
 .\n\nOrganisation: Emerging Writers' Festival and Hachette Australia\nLoca
 tion: Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.\nAward: AUD $10\,000
  prize money (donated by Hachette Australia)\; 12-month mentorship with a 
 Hachette Australia publisher\; first option to consider finished work for 
 publication. Long- and short-listed writers also eligible to apply for a C
 asa Morphosi Residency in Abruzzo\, Italy. No application fee.\nDuration: 
 12-month mentorship.\n\nMore: https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/riche
 ll-prize-2026/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://emergingwritersfestival.org.au/richell-prize-2026/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Australia\; must currently reside in Australia.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jamverse-jam-protocolized-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260731
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260801
SUMMARY:Deadline: Jamverse Jam (Protocolized worldbuilding contest)
DESCRIPTION:Protocolized's fourth contest invites artists and writers (stor
 ytellers\, poets\, comic artists) to build\, extend\, and connect the Jamv
 erse\, a shared near-future science fiction universe of four intersecting 
 story cycles (The Stockton Chronicles\, Legends and Ledgers\, UET-Trainver
 se\, and Zoothesia). ELIGIBILITY: open to anyone\; you may submit up to th
 ree in-universe worldbuilding artifacts\, and the same creator can win mul
 tiple prizes. REQUIREMENTS: each artifact must be under 1\,000 words (200-
 700 is the sweet spot) or a visual equivalent (comic panel\, poster\, adve
 rtisement\, etc.) and must include at least one element drawn from one of 
 the four sub-world 'toy boxes'\, a surprising 'traffic jam' (an emergent\,
  ah-ha consequence of a technology or protocol)\, and a relation to the th
 eme of Stigmergy (indirect coordination through modifying a shared environ
 ment). Artifact types can include comic panels\, job postings\, poems\, fo
 rum threads\, historic documents\, or encyclopedia entries. APPLY: submit 
 each artifact as a public Google Doc via the contest form\, including the 
 artifact\, a one-paragraph explanation of the worlds/toy-box components us
 ed and how the piece relates to stigmergy and explores a traffic jam\, and
 \, if AI was used\, documentation of your AI/LLM protocol. PRIZES: one USD
  1\,000 grand prize and at least 10 USD 200 prizes\, all published on the 
 Jamverse website. DEADLINE: 31 July 2026. Contest rules\, the current Jamv
 erse stories\, and starter materials are on the Jamverse website.\n\nOrgan
 isation: Protocolized (Summer of Protocols)\nLocation: Online / Worldwide\
 nAward: One USD 1\,000 grand prize plus at least 10 additional USD 200 pri
 zes\; winning artifacts are published on the Jamverse website. No entry fe
 e.\nDuration: One-off contest\; submit up to three artifacts by 31 July 20
 26\n\nMore: https://protocolized.summerofprotocols.com/p/jamverse-jam?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://protocolized.summerofprotocols.com/p/jamverse-jam?utm_source=ar
 tificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Online / Worldwide
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ucross-fellowship-native-american-writers-spring-2027@artificialnouveau
 .github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260715
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260716
SUMMARY:Deadline: Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers: Spring 202
 7
DESCRIPTION:The Ucross Fellowship for Native American Writers is a dedicate
 d fellowship within Ucross Foundation's residency program\, offering fully
  funded time and space to write on a working cattle ranch in the foothills
  of Wyoming's Bighorn Mountains. The fellowship provides living accommodat
 ions\, meals\, and work space at no charge\, with no application fee. One 
 Fellowship winner is selected per session\; all applicants also have the o
 ption of being considered for a general Ucross residency. ELIGIBILITY: ope
 n to Native American writers who are practicing contemporary writers curre
 ntly producing work in one or more genres\, including but not limited to f
 iction\, nonfiction\, poetry\, drama\, screenwriting\, playwriting\, and h
 ybrid forms\; and who are enrolled members of a state-recognized or federa
 lly-recognized Tribe\, Pueblo\, Nation\, Native Community\, Political Enti
 ty\, or Alaskan Native Village. The work sample and project description ar
 e the most significant features of the application. Unless the work is gen
 uinely interdisciplinary (the genres interconnect)\, applicants are encour
 aged to apply in a single primary discipline and submit a work sample and 
 project description emphasizing that discipline. Work-sample requirements:
  FICTION\, 20 pages of double-spaced fiction (a novel excerpt\, a story\, 
 several stories\, or a combination)\; NONFICTION\, 20 pages of double-spac
 ed nonfiction\; POETRY\, 10 pages of poetry (may be single-spaced)\; PLAYW
 RITING\, one complete play (production documentation may be included if re
 levant)\, noting the 20 pages for reviewers to read\; SCREENWRITING\, one 
 complete screenplay (production documentation may be included if relevant)
 \, noting the 20 pages for reviewers to read. All writing samples should i
 nclude your full name. The open call for the Spring 2027 session closes Ju
 ly 15\, 2026 (apply by 11:59 p.m. MT). A separate Ucross Fellowship for Na
 tive American Visual Artists is also offered.\n\nOrganisation: Ucross Foun
 dation\nLocation: Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA\nAward: Fully funded fellowship
  residency\; living accommodations\, meals\, and work space provided. No a
 pplication fee.\nDuration: Spring session (February through early June 202
 7)\; residencies range from two to six weeks\n\nMore: https://www.ucrossfo
 undation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&
 utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.ucrossfoundation.org/residency-program.html?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Sheridan\, Wyoming\, USA
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:accent-sisters-work-study-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260801
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260802
SUMMARY:Deadline: Accent Sisters Work-Study Fellowship Program 2026
DESCRIPTION:Accent Sisters offers a work-study fellowship for individuals p
 assionate about literature\, visual arts\, and community engagement. The f
 ellowship provides hands-on experience in a bookstore and a hybrid event s
 pace where exhibitions\, book launches\, film screenings\, educational pro
 grams\, and other public programming take place. It is ideal for someone l
 ooking to build a career in cultural institutions\, independent bookstores
 \, or art galleries. Fellows work closely with both the bookstore and gall
 ery teams\, gaining skills in book curation\, exhibit planning\, marketing
 \, and community outreach\, while developing a personal project that merge
 s these disciplines. WHAT YOU GET: opportunities to work with writers\, ar
 tists\, and creators\; a chance to pitch projects\, events\, and programs 
 for a diverse community\; free passes to all in-person events and classes\
 ; fellow discounts\; a showcase of your personal project toward the end of
  the fellowship\; one-on-one mentorship with the Accent team\; and a USD 6
 00 stipend. Open to upcoming seniors\, upcoming second-year graduate stude
 nts\, and recent graduates of undergraduate/graduate programs. Four tracks
  are offered and applicants generally choose one: Program Coordination & C
 ommunity Engagement\; Curation & Administration\; Graphic Design & Digital
  Asset Management\; or Marketing & Business Development. The fellowship ru
 ns 20 August to 20 December 2026 (part-time\, 3 days per week\, on-site re
 quired). Applications are reviewed from 15 July to 1 August 2026.\n\nOrgan
 isation: Accent Sisters\nLocation: 89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 
 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\, 3 days/week\, on-site work required)\nAward: S
 tipend of USD 600 (paid in two installments)\, plus free passes to all in-
 person events and classes\, fellow discounts\, and one-on-one mentorship.\
 nDuration: 20 August to 20 December 2026\; part-time\, 3 days per week\n\n
 More: https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialno
 uveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.accentaccent.com/page/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:89 5th Ave\, Suite 702\, New York\, NY 10003 (hybrid\; part-time\,
  3 days/week\, on-site work required)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cullman-center-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cullman Center Fellowships 2027-28 (New York Public Libra
 ry)
DESCRIPTION:The Cullman Center for Scholars and Writers at the New York Pub
 lic Library awards fifteen fellowships a year to writers and scholars (fic
 tion\, nonfiction\, poetry\, playwriting\, translation\, and academic rese
 arch) whose projects draw on the Library's collections. Fellows receive US
 D 90\,000\, an office\, and full research access for a September-May term 
 in residence. Open internationally. Deadline 25 September 2026\, 5pm EDT\;
  apply via the NYPL online application portal.\n\nOrganisation: Dorothy an
 d Lewis B. Cullman Center for Scholars and Writers\, New York Public Libra
 ry\nLocation: New York City\, United States. Open internationally\; fellow
 s work in residence at NYPL's Stephen A. Schwarzman Building.\nAward: USD 
 90\,000 stipend\, plus an office at the library\, full access to collectio
 ns\, and a September-May term. No application fee.\nDuration: September 20
 27 to May 2028\, in residence at the New York Public Library.\n\nMore: htt
 ps://www.nypl.org/about/fellowships-institutes/cullman-center-scholars-wri
 ters/fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.nypl.org/about/fellowships-institutes/cullman-center-schola
 rs-writers/fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:New York City\, United States. Open internationally\; fellows work
  in residence at NYPL's Stephen A. Schwarzman Building.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:woodward-newman-award-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20260901T212827Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: Woodward/Newman Award 2027-28 (Constellation Stage & Scre
 en)
DESCRIPTION:The Woodward/Newman Award\, started with the support of Joanne 
 Woodward\, Newman's Own Foundation and the Newman family\, is awarded annu
 ally by Constellation Stage & Screen to the best unpublished new play. The
  winner receives a USD 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) and a full
  production in the Mainstage season\, with housing and transportation prov
 ided. The contest format and submission fees have been eliminated: submiss
 ions are reviewed on an ongoing basis and are also considered for all seve
 n production slots in the season\, including TYA (theatre for young audien
 ces) shows. Full-length plays should run 75 to 135 minutes\; TYA shows ove
 r 40 minutes. Plays must be unpublished at submission (independently publi
 shed is acceptable)\; limit of two submissions per playwright per year\; m
 usicals may include demos. Plays submitted by midnight 31 August 2026 are 
 considered for the 2027-28 season\; later submissions roll to 2028-29. All
  submissions are kept on file for two years.\n\nOrganisation: Constellatio
 n Stage & Screen\nLocation: Bloomington\, Indiana\, USA. Open submissions 
 of unpublished plays\; production takes place in Bloomington.\nAward: USD 
 3\,000 cash prize (in lieu of royalties) plus a full production in Constel
 lation's Mainstage season\; housing and transportation provided. No submis
 sion fee.\nDuration: Winner announced by 15 May 2027\; production in the 2
 027-28 season. Submissions stay on file and under consideration for two ye
 ars.\n\nMore: https://seeconstellation.org/get-involved/submit-play/?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
